.TH VTS 1
.SH NAME
vts \- Plan 9 console daemon: VT100 rendering, session persistence, line editing in one 9P server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vts
[
.B -n
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vts
is a 9P file server that collapses what Unix splits across three tools
(st for VT100 rendering, tmux for session persistence, zsh for line
editing) into a single Plan 9 service. It posts at
.B /srv/vts
with the namespace
.IP
.EX
/                directory
/ctl             daemon control
/<sess>/         per session
/<sess>/ctl      per-session control
/<sess>/cons     viewer side: write keystrokes in, read them back
/<sess>/tty      shell side: bound over /dev/cons in the shell
/<sess>/ttyctl   shell side: bound over /dev/consctl; rawon/rawoff
/<sess>/cells    binary cell-diff stream
.EE
.PP
By default, vts starts one session named
.B 1
and spawns
.B rc(1)
inside it. rc's stdout is fed through a VT100 state machine into a
cell buffer.  Writes to
.B /<sess>/cons
deliver keystrokes to rc's stdin. Reads from
.B /<sess>/tty
is the same terminal seen from the shell's end, and is the reason the
shell can run
.IR bash (1)
with its own line editing. A read BLOCKS until a keystroke arrives
(a zero-length read would mean the terminal had closed) and a write
goes straight into the VT parser. It exists as a file separate from
.B cons
because a viewer and a shell face opposite ways: the viewer writes the
keystrokes the shell reads.
.PP
.I Vts
binds it over
.B /dev/cons
in a namespace of the shell's own, and that bind is load-bearing rather
than tidy: APE's
.I isatty
asks
.I fd2path
and matches a path ending in
.BR /dev/cons ,
so a pipe -- or this file opened as /mnt/1/tty -- is not a terminal
however well it behaves, and
.I bash
compiles its line editing out when it is told it has none.
.B /<sess>/ttyctl
is bound over
.B /dev/consctl
alongside it and takes
.B rawon
and
.BR rawoff ,
which is the whole of what Plan 9's consctl accepts and therefore the
whole of what APE's
.I tcsetattr
writes. Neither a file nor a pipe has a line discipline to switch, so
what it really does is turn
.IR vts 's
own line editor off.
.PP
.B /<sess>/cells
return a binary diff frame (header + changed cells); see
.IR vts-9p (5).
.PP
With
.B -n
no rc is spawned. Useful for testing the 9P interface in isolation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -n
Do not spawn a shell. The server still serves /srv/vts; writes to cons
are fed directly through the parser.
.PP
The shell is
.B $SHELL
if it is set and
.B /bin/rc
otherwise; a bare name without a slash is looked for in
.BR /bin .
.I Apexp-sh
sets SHELL=bash, so running
.I vts
from inside it is what puts bash within reach -- from a plain
.IR rc ,
bash is not in the namespace at all.
.SH CONTROL COMMANDS
Writes to
.B /srv/vts/ctl
accept newline-terminated commands:
.IP
.EX
new <name>     create a new session and spawn rc in it
kill <name>    destroy the session (sends 'kill' note to rc)
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
Start vts and attach a remote SSH user:
.IP
.EX
% vts &
% ssh -p 2222 glenda@localhost
glenda% vts-attach 1
.EE
.PP
List sessions:
.IP
.EX
% cat /srv/vts > /n/vts ; cat /n/vts/ctl
.EE
.PP
Create a second session:
.IP
.EX
% echo 'new dev' > /n/vts/ctl
% ls /n/vts
ctl  1  dev
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /srv/vts
9P service mount point
.TP
.B /sys/src/cmd/vts/
source
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vts-attach (1),
.IR rio (1),
.IR rc (1),
.IR thread (2),
.IR 9p (2)
.SH HISTORY
First written 2026.
.SH BUGS
.PP
Programs that gate features on
.IR isatty (3)
(htop, lazygit) still see a dumb pipe.  vts does not implement PTYs.
.PP
A killed session leaks its
.I Session
struct; freeing requires waiting for the reader proc to exit, which is
not implemented in this version.
.PP
Session-scoped scrollback and named-session persistence across vts
restarts are not implemented.
